// ?privateFireWeapon@Weapon@@QAE_NPBVObject@@PBUCoord3D@@0H1HHHPAH@Z
// partial score=0.1405 date=2026-09-29
// cl: /DNDEBUG /MD /O2 /Ob2 /GX-
// Open-BFME5: Weapon::privateFireWeapon, retail 0x001E9FD0 size 1253.
// Identity proven by ILT 0x00030A8A (?privateFireWeapon nine-arg) and ten
// matched callers. Frame matches retail (sub esp,0x20).
//
// Probe: ours 1134 vs retail 1253, 839 non-reloc diffs, 20 relocation drifts,
// first at +3. (Was 1138 / 843 / 21 on the previous banked spelling.)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Slot map, re-derived this session with a correct ESP model and now believed:
//
// Retail's steady-state ESP in the body is E-0x30 (sub esp,0x20 plus the four
// saved-register pushes ebx/ebp at +0x07/+0x0c and esi/edi at +0x5f/+0xf8).
// Every call in the body is callee-clean, so that value is constant from
// +0xf8 to the end.  Reading the argument homes off that base:
//
//   E+0x04 a1 source        copied to ebx at +0x08
//   E+0x08 a2 sourcePos     (never given a home; only pushed)
//   E+0x0c a3 victim        also ZEROED in its own home at +0x2b1 (scatter arm)
//   E+0x10 a4 victimId      (only pushed, in the fireWeaponTemplate call)
//   E+0x14 a5 victimPos     holds the scatter arm's target position, and the
//                           loop's hoisted element count at +0x275/+0x27c
//   E+0x18 a6               passed to fireWeaponTemplate as its 9th argument
//   E+0x1c a7               the DETONATION test at +0x189, and later the linear
//                           loop's induction variable at +0x1d0/+0x1f0
//   E+0x20 a8 extraBonusFlags  computeBonus's 2nd arg; also `one`/cacheOk
//                           (+0x09c/+0x0c3) and `reloaded` on two return paths
//   E+0x24 a9 projectileID  the `if (projectileID) *projectileID = 0` at +0x3a
//
// This confirms the earlier session's finding that this file's parameter
// NAMES are wrong (arg6 is the flag handed to fireWeaponTemplate, arg7 is the
// one tested for detonation) but it also confirms that renaming them COSTS
// bytes: re-measured this session on top of the loop change below, the honest
// rename gives 1148 bytes / 870 diffs against 1134 / 839.  Not applied; the
// slot map is recorded here and in the re_log evidence line instead.
//
// The fireWeaponTemplate call is settled too.  Its eleven pushes decode, in
// argument order, as: source, a2, m_wslot, m_curBarrel, a3, a4, &bonus,
// &bonus, a6, this, a9.  The 7th and 8th arguments are the SAME address
// because the linear-aim helper writes the aimed coordinate into the
// WeaponBonus's own storage, so the alias in the scatter/linear arms is
// retail behaviour and not a reconstruction artefact.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Linear-arm loop, decoded and re-spelled this session:
//
//   +01a6 mov esi,[ebp+4] ; +01a9/+01ac load begin/end ; +01af lea ecx,[esi+0x40]
//   +01b2..+01c2  (end-begin)/12 ; +01c4 je 0x294      (count==0 -> scatter arm)
//   +01ca xor edi,edi ; +01cc xor esi,esi ; +01ce jmp 0x1d4
//   +01d0 mov esi,[esp+0x4c]        <- loop head reloads the induction variable
//   +01d4..+01e9 (end-begin)/12 recomputed through ecx, NOT hoisted
//   +01eb mov edx,esi ; +01ed inc esi ; +01ee cmp edx,eax
//   +01f0 mov [esp+0x4c],esi ; +01f4 je 0x3c5
//   +01fa body ; +0x284 test edi,edi ; +0x289 je 0x1d0 (continue) ; +0x28f break
//
// So retail's loop is a rotated `for(;;) { if (i == count) break; BODY }` whose
// induction variable also has a home in a dead argument slot, and whose count
// is recomputed every iteration.  The outer `else if` tests the pointer
// inequality; the division at +0x1a6..+0x1c2 belongs to the loop, not the test.
//
// The banked spelling below is the closest of thirteen measured forms
// (1134 / 839).  Forms that reproduce retail's *recomputed* count, whether in
// the loop test, the modulo, the outer condition or all three, are all worse:
// 1196-1212 bytes and 874-931 diffs.  MSVC 7.1 hoists the expression in every
// spelling tried, so the recompute is not reachable from C++ here; and the
// rotated layout (test at the top of the loop, body at the bottom) is not
// produced either.  Both are recorded as disproved.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ruled out this session (each measured with tools/probe.py):
//   * loop form: while (i<n) 1138/843; do/while (i++!=n) 1136/840;
//     for(;;){if(i==n)break;} 1134/839; for(;;){if(i++==n)break;} 1140/849;
//     for(;;){if(i++==n)break;} with an inline count 1196/900.
//   * hoisting the count into `n` and using it in the modulo as well (M1)
//     1186/906.
//   * outer `else if` on the divided count instead of the pointer inequality
//     1134/849 (M3) and 1164/874 (R4).
//   * inverting the tail to `if (getRemainingAmmo(false) > 0) {...} else {...}`
//     (which is what retail's `ja` at +0x405 wants) 1136/842 - worse than the
//     `== 0` spelling, even though it is the only one that can emit `ja`.
//   * WeaponBonus initialiser spelling: assignment chain 1154/1007; six-slot
//     loop 1154/1008; the six stores written inline in the function body
//     instead of in the constructor 1134/839 (byte-identical to the ctor).
//   * the honest arg6/arg7 rename 1148/870 (see above).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Remaining blockers, both global register allocation, not statement order:
//
//   (a) The prolog.  Retail saves ebx,ebp,esi,edi in that order and assigns
//       ebx=source (+0x08), ebp=this (+0x1a), esi=victim (+0x63), edi=delay
//       (+0x10c).  This build saves ebp,edi,ebx and assigns ebp=this,
//       edi=source, ebx=victim, and spills `delay` to a dead argument home.
//       Retail has four saved registers; this build has three.  As a knock-on
//       the argument homes differ: retail keeps `now` in a1's home and
//       `delay` in edi, this build keeps both on the stack.
//
//   (b) The 119-byte size shortfall (1134 vs 1253) downstream of +0x1a1.
//       Retail emits 431 instructions against this build's 394 with the same
//       22 calls and the same 15 reloads of m_template; the extra 37
//       instructions are 10 extra esp-relative memory operands, i.e. more
//       redundant reloads, which is what (a) predicts.
//
// Neither is reachable from the source text tried here.  The Zero Hour twin
// is inputs/reference/CnC_Generals_Zero_Hour/GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Source/
// GameLogic/Object/Weapon.cpp:2481 (Weapon::privateFireWeapon) and agrees with
// this reconstruction everywhere BFME has not diverged - the ZH function has
// eight arguments and no linear-target arm, and BFME's `getDelayBetweenShots`
// has been hoisted to the top of the body and the delay-carrying tail
// restructured, both of which the disassembly confirms.
#include <math.h>
